<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mukteshvara Temple, Bhubaneswar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">10th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mukteshvara Temple is a small 10th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhubaneswar">Bhubaneswar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, built under the Somavamshi dynasty. Admired as a gem of Odishan architecture, it marks the transition to the mature <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> style and is known for its carved arched gateway (torana) and for reliefs of ascetics, animals and scenes from the Panchatantra fables.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mukteshvara-temple-bhubaneswar.jpg" alt="Mukteshvara Temple, Bhubaneswar" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robin Mohapatra · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muktesvar_Temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The temple is dated to 950–975 CE and is held to be the earliest work of the Somavamshi period. Most scholars place it after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parashurameshvara-temple">Parashurameshvara Temple</a> and before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmeswara-temple">Brahmeswara Temple</a> of 1060 CE. Percy Brown dated its construction to 950 CE, while K. C. Panigrahi placed it in 966 CE and proposed that the Somavamshi king Yayati I built it, though no historical evidence confirms this. Its design gathers the earlier developments of Odishan temple building and opens a century of experiment seen in later temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhubaneswar">Bhubaneswar</a> such as the Rajarani Temple and the Lingaraj temple.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The temple faces west and stands on a lower level among a group of temples, inside an octagonal compound wall with elaborate carvings. It has two main parts on a raised platform: the vimana over the sanctum, whose shikara rises 10.5 metres and bears four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nataraja">Natarajas</a> and four kirtimukhas, and the jagamohana, the hall in front. The pyramidal roof of the jagamohana was the first of its kind, and the temple is the earliest of the pithadeula type. A new ornament, the bho, a richly carved chaitya window crowned by a demon mask and dwarf figures, may have been developed here and became common in later Odishan temples.</p><p>The arched torana before the jagamohana, unique in the region, shows the influence of Buddhist architecture; its thick pillars carry carved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> and reclining women, and its sculptures range from scrolls to monkeys and peacocks. The red sandstone walls show lean sadhus and jewelled women, Ganga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a>, Gajalakshmi, Rahu and Ketu, and Lakulisa on the lintel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The name Mukteshvara means Lord of Freedom, from the cycle of births and deaths, and the temple is dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>. Many carvings show skeletal ascetics teaching or meditating, and some scholars link the name to a role as a centre of Tantric initiation. Niches on the compound wall hold Saraswathi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a> and Lakulisha, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashupata-shaivism">Pashupata</a> sect. According to tradition, childless women who bathe in the Marichi Kunda tank on the night before the Ashokashtami car festival will bear sons. The state's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Tourism holds a yearly three-day festival of Odissi dance in the temple grounds.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mukteshvara Temple, Bhubaneswar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The temples of Bhubaneswar reached maturity under their rule: the <b>Mukteshvara</b> temple and the great Lingaraja temple, with its soaring tower, date from this period, as does the Brahmeshvara temple built by Queen Kolavati.</blockquote><a href="/state/somavamshi-dynasty">Somavamshi dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
